module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularImmersion.Sequence
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.LocalCriterionNonNoetherian
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Koszul.FlatQuotient

/-!
# A sub-ideal cutting out the same fibre as a flat quotient cuts out the same subscheme

Let `A → B` be of finite presentation, let `q` be a prime of `B` with `p = A ∩ q`, and let
`J ⊆ I` be finitely generated ideals of `B` with `B_q/IB_q` flat over `A`.  If `I` is contained in
`J + pB` after localizing at `q` — that is, if `J` and `I` generate the same ideal of the fibre
`B_q/pB_q` — then `JB_q = IB_q`, and therefore `JB_g = IB_g` for some `g ∉ q`.

This is part (2) of Stacks 062Y and of Stacks 063W: the closed subscheme cut out by the sequence
produced from the fibre coincides with the given one in a neighbourhood of the point, as soon as
the given one is flat over the base there.

## Main results

* `Stacks.RingTheory.SpreadingOut.map_eq_map_atPrime_of_le_sup_map_comap`: the stalk statement,
  `IB_q = JB_q`.
* `Stacks.RingTheory.SpreadingOut.exists_notMem_map_eq_map_away_of_map_atPrime_eq`: an equality
  of extended ideals at a prime already holds away from a single element outside it.
* `Stacks.RingTheory.SpreadingOut.exists_notMem_map_eq_map_away`: the same after localizing away
  from a single element outside `q`.

## Implementation notes

The stalk statement is Stacks 046Y, the non-Noetherian criterion
`Module.Flat.injective_and_flat_quotient_range_of_injective_mapQ_maximalIdeal`, applied to the
surjection `u : B_q/JB_q → B_q/IB_q`.  Its hypotheses are that source and target be of finite
presentation over `B_q` — which is the finite generation of `J` and of `I` — that the target be
flat over `A_p`, and that `u` be injective modulo the maximal ideal of `A_p`, which is exactly the
hypothesis on the fibre.  No flatness of `B_q/JB_q` is needed: only the target has to be flat.

The fibre of a quotient is read off by `Ideal.smul_top_eq_map`:  the `A_p`-submodule
`𝔪_p • (B_q/K)` is the ideal of `B_q/K` generated by the image of `𝔪_p`, and `𝔪_p B_q = p B_q` by
`Stacks.RingTheory.SpreadingOut.map_maximalIdeal_eq_map_of_isLocalization`.  Membership of a class
in such an ideal is membership in the sum, which is `mem_map_quotient_iff` here, and the
hypothesis then gives `pB_q + IB_q ≤ pB_q + JB_q` directly.

The spreading out is elementary and needs no flatness: membership of a generator of `I` in `JB_q`
means that an element outside `q` carries it into `J`, by
`Stacks.RingTheory.SpreadingOut.algebraMap_quotientMap_eq_zero_iff`, and the product of those
elements over a finite generating set of `I` is the `g` sought.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 062Y](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/062Y)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 046Y](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/046Y)
* [Stacks Project, Lemma 31.22.8 (Tag 063W)](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/063W)
-/

@[expose] public section

